## 4. Row-Level Access Control (RLS-equivalent)

> **MySQL 8 has no native RLS. We implement RLS-equivalent in application middleware + DB user privileges.**

### 4.1 DB-level (SQL grants)
```sql
-- App user: scoped privileges
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE
  ON foodghor.products, foodghor.categories, foodghor.testimonials,
     foodghor.inquiries, foodghor.site_settings,
     foodghor.rate_limit_hits, foodghor.audit_log
  TO 'foodghor_app'@'localhost';

-- Read-only on admin_users (no direct create/delete via app)
GRANT SELECT, UPDATE(last_login_at, must_change_pw, password_hash)
  ON foodghor.admin_users
  TO 'foodghor_app'@'localhost';
```

### 4.2 Application-level (enforced in `Middleware/Auth.php`)

| Role | Permissions |
|------|-------------|
| **anonymous** | `GET` on public routes · `POST /inquiries` |
| **editor** | Everything above + full CRUD on products, categories, testimonials · update `status`/`notes` on inquiries · **cannot** touch `admin_users`, `site_settings`, or `audit_log` |
| **owner** | Everything editors can + read `audit_log` · manage `site_settings` · (provisioning of new admins is CLI-only) |

### 4.3 Row-visibility rules
- Public product queries: **always** `WHERE is_active = 1`
- Public category queries: **always** `WHERE is_active = 1`
- Public testimonial queries: **always** `WHERE is_active = 1`
- Admin queries: see all rows

### 4.4 Audit integrity
- `audit_log` is **append-only**. `DELETE` and `UPDATE` on this table are forbidden at the controller level even for `owner`.
- Editors mutating a row record `actor_id` + before/after diff in `metadata_json`.

**These rules MUST be enforced server-side. Never trust the frontend.**

